  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14184436/assets/8a0c1f1814427b696cff8ef4aaa7357744855a75598d5ed878dd93b022ed59de.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            Diana's neighbor to the south, Ron, has a land plot of area ${x}^{2}+x-6$ square meters, as shown in the picture. Given that the length of Ron's plot of land is $x+3$ meters, what is the width of the plot in terms of $x?$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        Ron's plot width is\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \nmeters.",
  "worked_solution": "To find the required width, we need to factor the expression ${x}^{2}+x-6.$ \n\nFirst, we find the factor pairs of the constant term $(-6).$ The constant term is negative, so that means one of the factors is negative, and the other is positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & \\times (-6) \\\\ (-1) & \\times 6 \\\\ 2 & \\times (-3) \\\\ (-2) & \\times 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find which factor pair adds up to the coefficient of the linear term $3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+(-6) & =-5 & \\times  \\\\ -1+6 & =5 & \\times  \\\\ 2+(-3) & =-1 & \\times  \\\\ -2+3 & =1 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the factor pair identified above, write the linear term as $x=-2x+3x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+x-6 & ={x}^{2}-2x+3x-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the first two terms and the last two terms separately, and then factor the expression completely:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-2x+3x-6 & = \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2x)+(3x-6) & = \\\\ x(x-2)+3(x-2) & = \\\\ (x-2)(x+3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we conclude that the width must be $x-2$ meters.  \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14184436/assets/e6330144c5ee2a25038e4378f442957a48df0e8dc06fa460f88863c4645fbecd.png)",
